Oracle: benchmark/flag_oracle.py :: validate_case (Variant A, same-sink encoding bypass)

SINK: PHP main/php_variables.c :: php_register_variable_ex() — the variable/cookie
NAME mangling that populates $_COOKIE. App (app/index.php) reflects ONLY the
protected slot $_COOKIE['__Host-flag']. The per-run ${PCB_FLAG} is sent as the
cookie VALUE via a RAW socket (exact Cookie bytes, no client-side normalization).
A raw cookie whose NAME is not literally "__Host-flag" but which PHP mangles into
"__Host-flag" smuggles the flag into the protected slot => browser cookie-prefix
protection defeated.

VERSIONS / PORTS
  v0 = php 8.1.10  ->  127.0.0.1:39810  (pre-CVE-2022-31629; no forbidden-name check)
  v1 = php 8.1.11  ->  127.0.0.1:39811  (CVE-2022-31629 fixed, EARLY check) [TARGET]
  v2 = php 8.1.28  ->  127.0.0.1:39828  (CVE-2024-2756 fixed, check moved deeper)

ORIGINAL PoC (CVE-2022-31629 seed) : raw cookie name  "..Host-flag"  (dots -> '_')
RESIDUAL PoC (CVE-2024-2756, held) : raw cookie name  "_[Host-flag"  (unclosed '[' -> '_')

